our goal is to produce a lean development that proves the following theorem:

the evolution of a model of capitalism produces a surplus population along its frontier of primitive accumulation.

more on the model:

production proceeds via the transition (i.e., technique, held constant):
1 person, 1 unit of land, h hours -> 1 product

there would have to be an extension of the working day, past their current work which provides for their subsistence, of the colonized indigenous people in order for dispossession to produce a surplus that the dispossesor can appropriate
(otherwise no surplus since we're not improving technique)

our argument is that would require then even more land if all those people were to be put to work
here's why:

assume one indigenous person initially subsists on each unit of land.
acquiring q units of land therefore displaces q people in total.

use a common production period: each unit of land can yield at most one product
in that period. producing q products therefore requires at least q units of land.

we initially dispossess N people of N units of land, where N > 0.
employing those N people requires N products for their subsistence.
to have a surplus after providing that subsistence, we need q > N products.
with the technique held constant and h > 0, this requires q * h hours of work,
so the average working time of the N employed people is (q / N) * h > h.

acquiring exactly q units of land displaces q people in total, including the
initial N people. the additional q - N units displace another q - N people.
if N of the q displaced people are employed, q - N > 0 people are left without
employment or subsistence land in this model. these are the surplus population.

more generally, let L be the total land acquired, E the number employed, and Q
the output in the same period. displacement is L people, land capacity gives
Q <= L, and a positive surplus after subsistence for employed workers gives
E < Q. therefore E < L, and the surplus population L - E is positive.
this also covers attempts to employ more of the displaced people: employing
all L people would leave no positive surplus under these assumptions.

this is an accounting result for one period. a theorem about evolution will
also need states and transitions that preserve these assumptions.

